  "slug": "forecasting-infectious-0468",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and publications extend forecasting with memory terms, simulation-informed training, and sequence-derived features.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is quantitative prediction of pathogen spread from observed data, with most items pairing a model class with explicit evaluation. A Caputo fractional-order hybrid SIR model adds memory effects and is tested out of sample on COVID-19 data from Bangladesh and the United States. Mantis 2 jointly trains on simulated and observed outbreak data to forecast six variables up to 16 weeks ahead, outperforming machine learning and statistical baselines. Sequence-derived graph-Laplacian features with machine learning estimate growth rates of emerging SARS-CoV-2 lineages from US and UK genomic data. A hybrid ODE-DynNet with multi-agent reinforcement learning reconstructs hidden epidemic states from incomplete reports and optimizes testing and isolation in Shenzhen. What is new is the range of extensions: memory terms, simulation-informed joint training, sequence-derived spectral features, and learned control under incomplete observation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
